<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-angles" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Angles</title>

  <definition xml:id="def-convex">
    <statement>
      <p>
        A subset of the plane is <term>convex</term> if it contains the whole segment
        that connects any two of its points.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    For example, one side of any line is a convex set, and triangles are convex sets.
  </p>

  <p>
    We do not define the term angle formally. Instead we say: There are things called
    <term>angles</term>. To each angle is associated:
    <ol>
      <li><p>a unique point <m>A</m>, called its <term>vertex</term>;</p></li>
      <li><p>two rays <m>[AB</m> and <m>[AC</m>, both starting at the vertex, and called
        the <term>arms</term> of the angle;</p></li>
      <li><p>a piece of the plane called the <term>inside</term> of the angle.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </p>

  <p>
    An angle is either a null angle, an ordinary angle, a straight angle, a reflex angle
    or a full angle. Unless otherwise specified, you may take it that any angle we talk
    about is an ordinary angle.
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="def-null-angle">
    <statement>
      <p>
        An angle is a <term>null angle</term> if its arms coincide with one another and
        its inside is the empty set.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <definition xml:id="def-ordinary-angle">
    <statement>
      <p>
        An angle is an <term>ordinary angle</term> if its arms are not on one line, and
        its inside is a convex set.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ordinary-angle">
    <caption>An ordinary angle <m>\angle BAC</m> with vertex <m>A</m></caption>
    <image width="40%">
      <description>Two rays from vertex A forming an ordinary angle, with arms towards B and C and the interior shaded</description>
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
          \fill[gray!20] (0,0) -- (3,0) arc[start angle=0, end angle=50, radius=3] -- cycle;
          \draw[-latex, thick] (0,0) -- (3,0)   node[right] {$B$};
          \draw[-latex, thick] (0,0) -- (1.93,2.3) node[above] {$C$};
          \filldraw (0,0) circle (2pt) node[below left] {$A$};
          \draw (0.6,0) arc[start angle=0, end angle=50, radius=0.6];
          \node at (0.85,0.3) {$\alpha$};
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <definition xml:id="def-straight-angle">
    <statement>
      <p>
        An angle is a <term>straight angle</term> if its arms are the two halves of one
        line, and its inside is one of the sides of that line.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <figure xml:id="fig-straight-angle">
    <caption>A straight angle at <m>A</m>: the arms point in opposite directions along a line</caption>
    <image width="55%">
      <description>Two opposite rays from A forming a straight angle, with the upper half-plane shaded as the interior</description>
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
          \fill[gray!20] (-3,0) rectangle (3,1.8);
          \draw[latex-latex, thick] (-3,0) -- (3,0);
          \filldraw (0,0) circle (2pt) node[below] {$A$};
          \node[right] at (3,0)  {$B$};
          \node[left]  at (-3,0) {$C$};
          \draw (0.7,0) arc[start angle=0, end angle=180, radius=0.7];
          \node[above] at (0,0.7) {$180^\circ$};
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <definition xml:id="def-reflex-angle">
    <statement>
      <p>
        An angle is a <term>reflex angle</term> if its arms are not on one line, and its
        inside is not a convex set.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <figure xml:id="fig-reflex-angle">
    <caption>A reflex angle at <m>A</m>: the interior is the larger region (more than <m>180^\circ</m>)</caption>
    <image width="40%">
      <description>Two rays from A with the large reflex interior shaded, showing more than 180 degrees</description>
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
          \fill[gray!20] (0,0) -- (3,0) arc[start angle=0, end angle=310, radius=3] -- cycle;
          \draw[-latex, thick] (0,0) -- (3,0)    node[right] {$B$};
          \draw[-latex, thick] (0,0) -- (1.93,-2.3) node[below] {$C$};
          \filldraw (0,0) circle (2pt) node[above left] {$A$};
          \draw[->] (1.5,0) arc[start angle=0, end angle=310, radius=1.5];
          \node at (-0.8, 0.5) {reflex};
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <definition xml:id="def-full-angle">
    <statement>
      <p>
        An angle is a <term>full angle</term> if its arms coincide with one another and
        its inside is the rest of the plane.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <definition xml:id="def-angle-notation">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose that <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m> are three noncollinear points. We
        denote the (ordinary) angle with arms <m>[AB</m> and <m>[AC</m> by
        <m>\angle BAC</m> (and also by <m>\angle CAB</m>). We shall also use the notation
        <m>\angle BAC</m> to refer to straight angles, where <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, <m>C</m>
        are collinear, and <m>A</m> lies between <m>B</m> and <m>C</m> (either side could
        be the inside of this angle).
      </p>
      <p>
        Sometimes we want to refer to an angle without naming points, and in that case we
        use lower-case Greek letters, <m>\alpha, \beta, \gamma</m>, etc.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <figure xml:id="fig-angle-notation">
    <caption>The angle <m>\angle BAC</m> with vertex <m>A</m> and arms <m>[AB</m> and <m>[AC</m></caption>
    <image width="40%">
      <description>Angle BAC labelled with vertex A at the bottom, ray AB pointing right and ray AC pointing upper-left</description>
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
          \draw[-latex, thick] (0,0) -- (3,0)     node[right] {$B$};
          \draw[-latex, thick] (0,0) -- (-1.5,2.5) node[above] {$C$};
          \filldraw (0,0) circle (2pt) node[below] {$A$};
          \draw (0.55,0) arc[start angle=0, end angle=121, radius=0.55];
          \node at (0.1,0.75) {$\angle BAC$};
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <exercises xml:id="exs-angles">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-angles-null">
      <statement>
        <p>An angle has arms that coincide with one another and its inside is the empty set.
        What type of angle is it?</p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>Review the definitions of the five types of angle.</p>
      </hint>
      <answer>
        <p>A null angle.</p>
      </answer>
      <solution>
        <p>By definition, a null angle is one whose arms coincide with one another and
        whose inside is the empty set. This matches the description exactly.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-angles-vertex-arms">
      <statement>
        <p>For the angle <m>\angle BAC</m>, identify the vertex and the two arms.</p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>The vertex is the unique point associated with the angle — the common starting
        point of the two rays.</p>
      </hint>
      <answer>
        <p>Vertex: <m>A</m>. Arms: <m>[AB</m> and <m>[AC</m>.</p>
      </answer>
      <solution>
        <p>The angle <m>\angle BAC</m> has vertex <m>A</m>. The two arms are the rays
        <m>[AB</m> and <m>[AC</m>, both starting at <m>A</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-angles-convex">
      <statement>
        <p>Is a line segment <m>[AB]</m> a convex set? Explain your answer.</p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>Pick any two points on the segment. Does the segment connecting them stay
        inside <m>[AB]</m>?</p>
      </hint>
      <answer>
        <p>Yes, a line segment is a convex set.</p>
      </answer>
      <solution>
        <p>A set is convex if it contains the whole segment connecting any two of its
        points. If we pick any two points on <m>[AB]</m>, the segment between them lies
        entirely within <m>[AB]</m>. So <m>[AB]</m> is a convex set.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-angles-ordinary-vs-straight">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the key difference between an ordinary angle and a straight angle?</p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>Think about the position of the arms in each case.</p>
      </hint>
      <answer>
        <p>In an ordinary angle the arms are not on one line; in a straight angle the
        arms are the two halves of one line.</p>
      </answer>
      <solution>
        <p>An ordinary angle has arms that are not on one line, and its inside is a convex
        set. A straight angle has arms that form the two halves of a single line (pointing
        in opposite directions), and its inside is one of the sides of that line.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

  </exercises>

</section>
